            <ArticleTitle>Equivalent reductions in body weight during the Beef WISE Study: beef's role in weight improvement, satisfaction and energy.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>298-310</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1002/osp4.118</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The objective of this randomized equivalence trial was to determine the impact of consuming lean beef as part of a high protein (HP) weight-reducing diet on changes in body weight, body composition and cardiometabolic health.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A total of 120 adults (99 female) with overweight or obesity (BMI: 35.7 ± 7.0 kg m(-2)) were randomly assigned to consume either a HP diet with ≥4 weekly servings of lean beef (B; n = 60) or a HP diet restricted in all red meats (NB; n = 60) during a 16-week weight loss intervention.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Body weight was reduced by 7.8 ± 5.9% in B and 7.7 ± 5.5% in NB (p &lt; 0.01 for both). Changes in percent body weight were equivalent between B and NB (mean difference: 0.06%, 90% confidence interval: (-1.7, 1.8)). Fat mass was reduced in both groups (p &lt; 0.01; B: 8.0 ± 0.6 kg, NB: 8.6 ± 0.6 kg), while lean mass was not reduced in either group. Improvements in markers of cardiometabolic health (total cholesterol, low-density lipoprotein cholesterol, triglycerides and blood pressure) were not different between B and NB.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Results of this study demonstrate that HP diets - either rich or restricted in red meat intakes - are effective for decreasing body weight and improving body composition and cardiometabolic health.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
